  "content": {
    "id": "v2-1f1e9-p35-practice-slot-3-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "A curve has gradient function $\\dfrac{\\sin x}{1+\\cos^2x}$ and passes through $\\left(0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$.\n\nUse $u=\\cos x$ to find its equation.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$y=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}-\\tan^{-1}(\\cos x)$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}y&=\\int\\frac{\\sin x}{1+\\cos^2x}\\,dx\\\\&=-\\int\\frac{1}{1+u^2}\\,du\\\\&=-\\tan^{-1}u+C\\\\&=-\\tan^{-1}(\\cos x)+C\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstitute $\\left(0,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi}{2}&=-\\tan^{-1}(1)+C\\\\&=-\\frac{\\pi}{4}+C\\\\C&=\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\\\\\therefore y&=\\frac{3\\pi}{4}-\\tan^{-1}(\\cos x)\\end{aligned}$$\n$$\\begin{aligned}u&=\\cos x\\\\u^2&=\\cos^2x\\\\\\frac{du}{dx}&=-\\sin x\\\\du&=-\\sin x\\,dx\\\\dx&=\\frac{du}{-\\sin x}\\end{aligned}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": null
  },
